<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Dress for the Weather.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend simple descriptions of people, places, and things around them. Interactive: Participate in call-and-response activities, responding appropriately to cues in songs or stories.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend essential weather-related words (e.g., "rainy" or "sunny"). Can follow simple descriptions of people in different weather conditions (e.g., "It's sunny. He is hot!"). Can participate in call-and-response songs, stories, and chants with appropriate cues (e.g., "How's the weather?" "It is rainy!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a "Weather Song" using Total Physical Response (TPR). The teacher sings simple phrases like "How's the weather? How's the weather?" and models actions (e.g., pretending to shiver for "cold," fanning for "hot"). Students listen and imitate the actions. Teacher holds up flashcards of sun, rain, cloud, wind and says, "Look! Sun!" "Look! Rain!" eliciting listening comprehension and linking visuals to words.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new vocabulary using large, colorful flashcards and clear, slow pronunciation. For each card (e.g., "sunny," "rainy," "windy," "cloudy," "coat," "umbrella," "hat," "boots"), the teacher shows the image, says the word twice, and asks students to listen and repeat chorally. The teacher specifically models the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "rainy," and points to the initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds when saying "windy." The teacher uses gestures for adjectives (e.g., waving hands for "windy," spreading arms wide for "sunny").
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary through simple listening questions and pointing.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a sunny day: "Is this rainy or sunny?" (Students listen and point to "sunny" flashcard or say "Sunny!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a child wearing a coat: "Does he wear a coat when it's hot?" (Students listen and shake heads "No" or say "No.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short weather report for children, accompanied by large, clear picture cards depicting different weather conditions and corresponding clothing items. The audio will feature simple sentences using adjectives + nouns (e.g., "It's a sunny day.") and present simple (e.g., "I wear my hat.").
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehending essential weather-related words and following simple descriptions of people in different weather conditions.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher plays the audio once. "Listen. What are they talking about? Weather or food?" (Students listen and point to "Weather" flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> Teacher plays the audio again, pausing after each weather description. "Listen: 'It's a rainy day. I need my umbrella.' " (Students listen and point to the picture of rain and an umbrella). "Listen: 'It's a sunny day. I like sunny days!' " (Students listen and point to the picture of the sun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> Teacher makes a simple statement while students look at the matching picture. Teacher plays audio: "It's cold!" Teacher points to a sunny picture. "Is it cold?" (Students listen and say "No" or shake heads). Then, teacher points to a cold, snowy picture and says, "It's cold!" "Is it cold?" (Students listen and say "Yes").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day," "windy day"), present simple descriptions, and WH-questions ("What's the weather like?"). Vocabulary: sun, rain, sunny, rainy, warm, cold, cloudy, windy, coat, hat, umbrella, boots, jacket, wear, like. Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "rainy," initial /w/ and final /i/ in "windy." Pragmatic: Describing the weather and responding to questions.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Draw (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher dictates simple weather descriptions. "Listen carefully. Draw a sunny day." "Draw a person wearing a hat." "Draw a rainy day. Draw an umbrella." Students listen to the instructions and draw on their mini-whiteboards or activity sheets. The teacher walks around, observes, and provides feedback on their listening accuracy by checking the drawings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Match (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher shows students two columns of pictures: one with weather conditions (e.g., sunny, rainy, windy) and another with clothing items (e.g., hat, umbrella, jacket). The teacher reads sentences aloud: "When it's windy, I wear my jacket." "When it's sunny, I wear my hat." Students listen and draw lines to match the weather condition to the appropriate clothing item. The teacher repeats sentences as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Call-and-Response Weather Wheel (Pair Interaction/Peer Check - Teacher-led):</b> The teacher uses a large "Weather Wheel" with different weather pictures. The teacher spins the wheel and asks "What's the weather like?" Students listen and respond chorally: "It is [sunny/rainy/windy/cloudy]!" The teacher then asks individual students to listen and point to the correct picture. For an added layer of listening, the teacher might say, "It's [windy]!" and students have to listen and point to the corresponding image on the wheel.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Dress for the Weather!" Guessing Game (Listening-focused activity).</b>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher prepares a set of large weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, cold, hot) and clothing flashcards (coat, hat, umbrella, boots, jacket, scarf). The teacher describes a weather condition and what someone is wearing, and students listen to guess the weather.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher secretly chooses a weather card and a corresponding clothing card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says: "Listen. I wear a hat today. I like sunny days! What's the weather like?" (Focus on "sunny day" as the listening cue).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the description ("I wear a hat today," "I like sunny days") and the question ("What's the weather like?"). They then listen and call out the weather, e.g., "Sunny!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher repeats with other examples: "I wear my boots and my coat. It's a rainy day! What's the weather like?" (Students listen and say "Rainy!"). The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /r/ in "rainy."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If time permits, the teacher can invite a student to come to the front, choose a weather card, and describe it (e.g., "It's cold. I wear a scarf."). Other students listen and guess. This promotes active listening for descriptions and preferences.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' ability to listen and respond accurately to weather descriptions and questions during the "Dress for the Weather!" game and other listening tasks.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Circle."</b> Students are given a worksheet with pairs of images (e.g., a sunny day vs. a rainy day, a coat vs. an umbrella). The teacher reads a simple description or question twice (e.g., "It's a windy day. Circle the windy picture." or "When it's cold, I wear a jacket. Circle the jacket."). Students listen carefully and circle the correct image.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies/points to 4 out of 5 weather or clothing items described orally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Appropriate Response:</b> Student participates in call-and-response activities, responding with correct words (e.g., "sunny," "rainy") when cued.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student follows simple, 1-step audio instructions (e.g., "Draw an umbrella.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather word did you hear today? Draw it!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the picture that matches 'I like sunny days!'"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather word we heard today? Say it!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you listen and show me 'rainy'?" (Students point to a flashcard or draw).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening-focused warm-up with TPR and songs effectively engaged students and introduced key vocabulary. The "Listen and Draw" activity was particularly good for assessing individual listening comprehension at this grade level. Some students struggled with differentiating between 'windy' and 'cloudy' during the audio tasks, indicating a need for more distinct pronunciation practice and visual cues for these specific words. I need to reinforce the /w/ and /i/ sounds in "windy." The call-and-response was successful for general comprehension.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> For Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), I will use short, simple texts featuring the same weather vocabulary ("sunny," "rainy," "windy," "cloudy," "coat," "umbrella," "hat," "boots") and grammatical structures (adjectives + nouns, present simple) introduced in this listening lesson. Students will recognize the words visually, building on their auditory comprehension and preparing them for reading within the familiar "Dress for the Weather" theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>